fact WorkItem(id: "WI-935", description: some(value: "Make Vec3 provide VectorSpace for real — blocked on a MEASURED short-name collision, not on the arithmetic.\n\nSTATE AFTER WI-931. 'fact VectorSpace[Vec3, Float]' is GONE from rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/geometry.anthill. It was never backed: VectorSpace declares FUNCTIONAL members (vec_add(a, b) -> V, vec_sub, vec_scale, vec_zero) while anthill.geometry implements the vector operations RELATIONALLY (rule vec_add(?a, ?b, ?c) :- ...), and WI-818 settled that a rule is not backing. It read as satisfied only because Vec3 is a 6.3 free-standing entity, which emitted no SortInfo and so was skipped by check_provider_operations as if abstract (WI-928). anthill-core/tests/include/algebra_spec_test.rs now asserts the ABSENCE and names this ticket, so restoring the fact flips that assertion back.\n\nTHE OBVIOUS FIX WAS TRIED AND MEASURED, AND IT REGRESSES. Writing 'sort Vec3 { entity Vec3(x: Float, y: Float, z: Float); operation vec_add(a: Vec3, b: Vec3) -> Vec3 = Vec3(x: a.x + b.x, ...) ... }' works on its own terms: the four bodies evaluate (11.0 / 33.0 / -18.0 / 6.0 / 0.0 on the obvious inputs), check_provider_operations is satisfied, and the whole workspace suite stayed GREEN -- 4010 passed. It also SILENTLY BREAKS THE RELATIONAL SURFACE. Driven, on a rule body doing 'import anthill.geometry.{vec_add}' and calling vec_add(a, b, ?c):\n  before the operations exist: 1 solution\n  after:                       0 solutions, no diagnostic\nThe FULLY QUALIFIED anthill.geometry.vec_add still resolves to 1 solution either way, so the rules themselves are intact -- what moves is the IMPORTED SHORT NAME, which binds to the new Vec3.vec_add/2 (arity 2) instead of the namespace rule vec_add/3. No existing test caught it: vec3_ops_test::vec_ops_callable_from_user_rule imports exactly this name and asserts only that the rule LOADS, so it passed throughout. That rule now has a driven peer in wi931_free_standing_provider_backing_test::the_relational_vec_rules_keep_their_short_name, which asserts the 1 solution.\n\nWHAT MUST BE DECIDED FIRST -- the naming, and it is a language question before it is a geometry one:\n (a) Is a namespace-level rule 'vec_add/3' and a sort member 'Vec3.vec_add/2' allowed to coexist under one short name at all? If yes, the import must either RESOLVE BY ARITY or be an AMBIGUITY ERROR -- what it must not do is silently pick one, which is what it does today. Note this is the same family as WI-907 (an ambiguous dotted head ends the ladder) and WI-933 (a declaration that silently does nothing), and the fix probably belongs with them rather than here.\n (b) Or do the two spellings get distinct names? e.g. move the relational clauses to their own namespace, or rename them -- but they are the SMT-facing surface (result as last positional per the smt-gen convention) and the algebraic laws in geometry.anthill (vec_add_comm, vec_add_assoc, vec_add_identity, vec_sub_def, vec_scale_distrib_v, vec_scale_distrib_s, vec_scale_assoc, vec_scale_identity) are all written against the 3-arity form, so a rename touches every law.\nMEASURED SCOPE NOTE, which makes (b) cheaper than it looks: NOTHING outside stdlib/anthill/geometry.anthill consumes these names. Grepped across examples/, anthill-todo/, anthill-smt-gen/ and the .anthill tree -- the only readers are the laws in that same file and vec3_ops_test.\n\nACCEPTANCE: Vec3 provides VectorSpace and the four members evaluate through the spec; the relational clauses still answer through whatever name they end up with, DRIVEN (a resolve, not a load); the ambiguity question in (a) is answered explicitly in docs/kernel-language.md or handed to the ticket that owns it; algebra_spec_test's assertion is flipped back with its comment updated; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-931 (2026-08-02), during its /code-review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T19:00:21Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-935", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (commit 1a8435dd) as option (c) -- a THIRD option the ticket did not list, and the two it did list both rested on a false premise.\n\nTHE PREMISE. The ticket framed this as \"may vec_add/3 and Vec3.vec_add/2 coexist under one short name\" and offered (a) adjudicate the collision or (b) rename. Both assume the two spellings must coexist. They must not: a rule-less bodied operation IS its own relation, and a hand-written clause of the same functor SUPPRESSES the derived view (design 3.3 precedence, stated in three places in the code). The relational clauses were a hand-rolled instance of what the machinery generates -- written in April by WI-137, before WI-138 lifted the shape to a spec and before WI-669 derived the reading. So the fix was to finish the WI-138 lift, not to adjudicate anything. Option (a) is handed off as WI-939, unanswered but no longer blocking.\n\nWHAT THE TICKET GOT WRONG ABOUT ITS OWN EVIDENCE. It called the relational clauses \"a working relational surface\". MEASURED: they answered 1 CONDITIONAL solution with ?c UNBOUND and the whole body residual. They computed nothing -- the residual was the point (Z3 consumed it). Both tests asserting them checked len()==1, which the residual satisfied. So the thing this ticket was protecting was never what it said it was, and the post-lift behaviour (1 DEFINITE solution, Vec3(11,22,33)) is strictly better than anything that ever existed here.\n\nWHAT IT COST, which the ticket did not anticipate: THREE resolver fixes, because deleting the clauses exposed that the derived reading did not work outside a prove run -- WI-937 (eponymous entities failed to materialize; the bridge aborted), WI-938 (no arity+1 view at resolve time), WI-941 (nullary ops never folded). Each was found only by DRIVING the goal; each loaded clean.\n\n/code-review CAUGHT THE ONE THAT MATTERED. My first cut shipped the change with the clauses deleted and no arity+1 view, and I reported the collision as \"gone\". It was -- and the pre-existing user spelling (import anthill.geometry.{vec_add}) went 1 -> 0 silently, reproducing by another route the exact regression WI-931 refused to ship. My control test asserted try_resolve_symbol(\"anthill.geometry.vec_add\").is_none() -- the FULLY QUALIFIED name -- while the hazard lives on the IMPORT path, and no test in the file imported a geometry name at all. Same class of error as the one WI-931 caught: checking a name instead of driving it.\n\nALSO FROM THE REVIEW, all real, all fixed: a spec headline of mine that contradicted the paragraph two lines below it (backing IS reachable via an inherited spec default); an algebra_spec_test assertion that was carrier-AGNOSTIC under a carrier-specific name (!rules_by_functor(spec).is_empty() passes for any carrier -- now uses common::sort_provisions); a \"provision is WITHDRAWN\" banner sitting directly above the test asserting it is present; the Ring-qualification inventory missing the two names my own new law added; 6.3s and scala-forward-mapping.md worked examples still naming Vec3 as free-standing; and a comment of mine claiming realization.anthill carries multi-line -: rules when it has ZERO.\n\nMEASURED SCOPE NOTE FROM THE TICKET WAS RIGHT AND INCOMPLETE: nothing outside geometry.anthill consumes the vec names, and the 8 laws are cited by no proof. But the CONSUMERS of the file shape were missed -- scaland asserts geometry entity/rule COUNTS (3 tests re-pointed) and cpp-gen pins the emitted struct (Vec3 is now the first stdlib type of the eponymous-with-operations shape WI-931 fixed cpp-gen for). scaland Bootstrap emits enum Vec3: case Vec3(...) plus a separate Ops trait for it and DROPS the bodies -> WI-940.\n\nSPEC: the functional-relation paragraph documented f(args, result) = eq(f(args), result), a form that can never answer. Rewritten around unify. Added: a NAMESPACE-level operation backs nothing (measured), and backing conformance is checked by SHORT NAME ONLY -- wrong arity, wrong return type and swapped params all load clean (measured, unenforced).\n\nSTILL OPEN, none of it done here: the generic route (requires VectorSpace[V, F]) reportedly loads clean then dies OperationBodyMissing -- reported by review, NOT verified by me, and it would be the first generic consumer of this lift; vec_sub_def has no body verification (an inert law with invented names loads clean); cpp-gens collect_entity_deps ignores operation signature types; lf1s realization.anthill points lift/lower at a C++ namespace and header that do not exist in the tree.\n\nTESTS: 8 in vec3_ops_test (4 driving values, 2 controls, 2 name checks), algebra_spec_test flipped and made carrier-aware, wi931 suite Vec3 family inverted. Controls MEASURED not asserted: restoring the namespace clause fails exactly one test; adding it inside sort Vec3 fails exactly one other; backing out WI-941 fails only zero_r. Full workspace exit 0. scaland core at its pre-existing baseline (8 failed / 232 passed, all downstream of an unrelated cpp_std parse break that predates this work).", at: "2026-08-02T19:00:03Z")
